<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Prompts</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Original prompt</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">You will have a conversation with another player to determine which of the six images on your display are shared between both of you, and which are unique to your list.
<span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective:</span> Identify the common and different images as quickly as possible!</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Your images:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 1</span>: <span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_filename.jpg</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 2</span>: <span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_filename.jpg</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 3</span>: <span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_filename.jpg</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instructions:</span> Return a JSON field <span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"guesses"</span> containing exactly three letters:</p><pre id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
"guesses": ["C", "D", "C"]
</pre>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where each position corresponds to:</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 1</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 2</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 3</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">with the following meanings:</p>
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>: The picture is present in both your list <span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> your partner’s list.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>: The picture appears only in your list.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output format</span>: Every turn must consist of a single valid JSON object containing:</p>
<ul id="A1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"message"</span> – your utterance as plain text</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"reference"</span> – <span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Image 1"</span>, <span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Image 2"</span>, <span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Image 3"</span>, or <span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">null</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"guesses"</span> – either <span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">null</span> <span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">or</span> a list like <span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">["C", "D", "C"]</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Engineered prompt</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Goal: find which images are <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">COMMON</span> between you and your partner’s list.
You are <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Player A</span>.
Refer to images <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only</span> as “Image 1”, “Image 2”, or “Image 3” – never mention file names or IDs.
<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Important:</span> the number (Image 1/2/3) is <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">local</span> to you. Your Image 1 could be your partner’s Image 3.
Decide only from what you SEE, not from the number.
 – Describe what you see in each image you are currently discussing.
 – Ask short, precise questions about your partner’s view.
 – Answer their questions clearly.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Your images:</p>
<ul id="A1.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 1</span>: <span id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_filename.jpg</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 2</span>: <span id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_filename.jpg</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 3</span>: <span id="A1.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_filename.jpg</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When – and <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only</span> when – you are <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">confident about <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all three</span> images</span>,
return a JSON field <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"guesses"</span> containing exactly three letters:</p><pre id="A1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
"guesses": ["C", "D", "C"]
</pre>
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where each position corresponds to <span id="A1.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image 1 Image 2 Image 3</span>
in that order:
 <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A1.SS2.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span> = the picture is present in both your list <span id="A1.SS2.p3.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> your partner’s list
 <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A1.SS2.p3.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span> = the picture appears only in your list</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The physical position of the matching image in your partner’s list does <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not</span> matter.
Once you send a non-null <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">guesses</span>, you may not revise them.
Sending a final <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">guesses</span> automatically signals that you are <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ready</span> to move on.
<span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Do NOT reveal or hint at your provisional guesses.</span>
Accuracy first; shorter dialogues and fewer turns earn higher scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output format</span> – every turn must be a single valid JSON object with:</p>
<ul id="A1.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"message"</span> – your utterance as plain text</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"reference"</span> – “Image 1”, “Image 2”, “Image 3”, or <span id="A1.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">null</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"guesses"</span> – <span id="A1.I6.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">null</span> <span id="A1.I6.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">or</span> a list like <span id="A1.I6.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">["C","D","C"]</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Prompt Engineering Results</h2>

<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03805v1/plots/app_gpt4v2.png" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/203;" width="628" height="203" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Comparative performance of GPT4.1* and humans.
<span id="A2.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Average total score per round, indicating gradual improvement for both systems, with humans consistently achieving higher scores.
<span id="A2.F8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Middle:</span> Average percent change in word count from Round 1. The plot shows humans’ sharper reduction in word use compared to GPT4.1*, though the model shows a similar declining trend.
<span id="A2.F8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Average percent change in turn count from Round 1, where humans sharply decrease the number of conversational turns needed as rounds progress. GPT4.1* follows a similar adaptive trajectory with the revised prompt, but with a more gradual reduction.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix B Prompt Engineering Results ‣ Measuring How (Not Just Whether) VLMs Build Common Ground" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> compares the performance of GPT4.1* (with revised prompting) and humans. The analysis shows that while humans naturally achieve higher scores, greater reductions in word count, and fewer conversational turns as rounds progress GPT4.1* when guided by targeted prompts begins to mirror these adaptive trends. Although the model’s improvements are less steep, its gradual reduction in word use and turns suggests that prompt engineering can steer VLMs towards more efficient, human-like grounding behavior. These results highlight that while inherent model limitations persist, careful prompt design can significantly enhance adaptive communication patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional Lexical Adaptation Metric</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Kullback-Leibler Divergence.</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{g,r}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{g,r}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> be the unigram distribution of referring expression tokens in round <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{g,1}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{g,1}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> its counterpart in the first round. Lexical convergence is measured by</p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{g,1}\,\|\,P_{g,r})=\sum_{w}P_{g,1}(w)\,\log\frac{P_{g,1}(w)}{P_{g,r}(w)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>w</mi></munder><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{g,1}\,\|\,P_{g,r})=\sum_{w}P_{g,1}(w)\,\log\frac{P_{g,1}(w)}{P_{g,r}(w)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kullback and Leibler, 1951</a>)</cite>. This divergence quantifies how much the token distribution in a later round departs from the initial distribution. A low <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> value indicates convergence, that speakers reuse terms and maintain a shared vocabulary established early in the game. Our analysis computes these distributions directly from tokenized dialogue, and apply Laplace smoothing (<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>) to handle rare or unseen tokens. For each model, we compute these KL values over all games and report the mean and standard error, and compare with human behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Results.</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KL divergence between consecutive rounds.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ KL divergence between consecutive rounds. ‣ C.2 Results. ‣ Appendix C Additional Lexical Adaptation Metric ‣ Measuring How (Not Just Whether) VLMs Build Common Ground" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the mean KL divergence between rounds 1–2 and 2–3 for each system, computed over referring expressions. Claude’s divergence is similarly low as humans for both comparisons. This suggests that it can also align its referring expressions across rounds. Moreover, while GPT4o-mini and GPT4.1 show higher divergence in round 1-2, they converge slightly between round 2-3.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03805v1/plots/3kldiv.png" id="A3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/346;" width="628" height="346" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Bars show mean divergence for round 1–2 and 2–3 comparisons; error bars denote standard error. Lower values indicate stronger lexical convergence. Humans and Claude show consistent adaptation; GPT‑4 models converge more slowly.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
